  "updated": "2026-08-07T00:18:11.073829+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Heat and humidity continues into Friday, with \nonly a few afternoon showers or thunderstorms.\n\nLatest model guidance does not show much need for changes to the\nforecast for the remainder of today and tomorrow. As typical\nwith convection, even hi-res models struggle with timing,\nplacement and coverage. We have already seen a few showers\ndevelop across areas further east (ie: Boston area to the North\nShore) than was previously anticipated. With the SW flow, the\nsouth coast sea breeze is moving northward and thus expect\nshower chances to remain north of this boundary as the\nafternoon/evening goes on. We do have a decent amount of Mixed\nLayer CAPE (MLCAPE) on the order of 2000 J/kg, but mid level\nlapse rates are very meager, so not expecting any substantial\nthunderstorm development. Convection will wane with sunset. With\nthe very moist airmass, we will again see the development of\nstratus and fog later tonight, especially across the southern\nhalf of SNE. Like today, those clouds and fog will \"burn\" off\ntomorrow morning. No real airmass change, though if anything, it\nwill be a degree or two warmer. 925mb temperatures today were in\nthe 23-24C range, and that rises to 25-26C on Friday. When\ncombined with dewpoints in the 70s, heat index (feels like)\ntemperatures will top out well into the 90s, if not low 100s.\nThus the Heat Advisory will continue. There will be a warm layer\naloft (above roughly 850mb/5000ft) that will act as a lid to\nkeep convection at bay. Though an isolated afternoon shower or\nthunderstorm is possible, they should be more the exception and\nlikely contained to far western areas where the hilly terrain\nwill help focus convective development. Guidance is a little\nunclear for Friday night, but we might see some late evening\nconvection move in from the west. Just kept with NBM PoPs\nshowing roughly 20-40% PoPs overnight for the CT River Valley\nand points east. \n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE2...Cool front brings scattered storms Saturday. \nLess humidity levels and dry weather Sunday but daily \nshower/storm chances resume early to midweek.\n\nAll in all a general sameness to the weather pattern day-by-day \nthrough Saturday, with warm temperatures in the 80s with very high \nhumidity levels, to go along with daily shower and thunderstorm \nchances following the diurnal cycle. Saturday offers a better shot a \na bit more thunderstorm coverage with a cool front, although the \nrisk for organized severe weather looks minimal, kept in check by \nweak windfields aloft leading to deep layer shear magnitudes in the \n25-30 kt range and generally slower SWly storm motions. This front \nwill offer somewhat lower humidity levels, basically knocking \ndewpoints down from current \"oppressive\" values in the low to mid \n70s to \"humid\" mid/upper 60s levels. Storms would be capable of \nlightning and heavy rain, with the strongest of those storms capable \nof frequent lightning and localized downdrafts capable of sporadic \nwind damage, but those should be a very small subset of the storms.  \n\nWeak subsidence and marginally lower dewpoints could limit or \nmitigate shower/storm chances on Sunday, although still with the \nusual summer warmth. The risk for daily shower and thunderstorm \nchances also increases early to mid next week as the Northeast \nstates become more influenced by a somewhat stronger belt of \nwesterly midlevel flow. With stronger winds aloft with that comes a \nsomewhat better chance for storms becoming strong/severe but the \ntiming is still uncertain. \n\nStayed fairly close to NBM forecast values during this period.",
